# Run 28: CAN ONE BOX CARRY THE EVENT? 83 arrivals/s — the real event rate,
# 50,000 respondents in ~10 minutes — over 4 generators against a SINGLE
# 12c-24g box (scratch web scaled to 1 instance, manual scaling), branded
# deck, bucket assets, healthy Key Value.
#
# Why this is worth a run: the case for two boxes rests on run 21, which
# measured one box at ~2 s medians at 80/s. Run 26 then showed that every
# measurement in that range was taken through a free-tier Key Value pinned at
# 100% CPU — with the cache failing OPEN, so the page cache missed and every
# request re-rendered. The same fleet on a healthy store ran 80/s at a 161 ms
# median with journeys at pure think-time and zero dropped iterations. So one
# box has never been measured under fair conditions.
#
# Clean here => the event needs ONE box, which halves the compute bill and
# removes the second instance (and its R2 dependency) from the critical path
# for Thursday. Two boxes then become headroom, not a requirement.
# Saturated => two boxes are genuinely needed and run 27 already proved them.
